      "The preferred was issued on August 8, 2019 at the closing of the Anadarko merger, per Occidental's Form 8-K of that date; April 30, 2019 is the date of the commitment, not of issuance. Occidental Series A terms (100,000 shares, $100,000 liquidation value per share, 8% accruing, 9% on accrued and unpaid amounts, warrant on 80.0 million common shares at $62.50, $10 billion total) from Occidental's Form 425 of April 30, 2019 on SEC EDGAR. The liquidation ranking ($105,000 per share plus accrued dividends, after satisfaction of all liabilities to creditors and before common) and the dividend blocker on common, junior and parity stock from the Certificate of Designations filed as Annex A to the Berkshire securities purchase agreement, Exhibit 10.1 to the April 30, 2019 Form 8-K. From Occidental's FY2025 Form 10-K (filed February 18, 2026): $679 million of preferred dividends paid in 2025, no redemptions that year, approximately $8.5 billion of face value outstanding at the filing date, the 8%/9% declaration language, the permanent step to 9% after arrears are cleared, the mandatory redemption at a 10% premium for every dollar distributed to common above $4.00 per share on a trailing 12-month basis, the bar on voluntary redemption before August 2029 and the 5% premium after it. From the Form 10-Q for the quarter ended June 30, 2026 (filed August 5, 2026): 84,897 preferred shares issued, carrying value $8,287 million, $2,000 per share quarterly dividend, $170 million deducted per quarter. Additional tier 1 eligibility criteria (perpetual, unsecured, subordinated to depositors, general creditors and subordinated debt holders, no step-up, five-year minimum before call, prior Board approval to redeem, full discretion to cancel distributions) from 12 CFR 217.20, 2025 edition, via govinfo. Non-cumulative language from Comerica's Certificate of Designations for its 5.625% Series A (Exhibit 3.1 to the Form 8-K filed May 26, 2020). The June 10, 2025 redemption notice for all 4,000 Series A shares at $1,000 per depositary share effective July 1, 2025, the $400 million reclassification from shareholders' equity to other liabilities, and the August 11, 2025 issuance of 400,000 shares of 6.875% Series B from Comerica's Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2025. Bank of America figures ($25,992 million preferred and $317,816 million long-term debt at December 31, 2025, $1.4 billion of preferred dividends declared in 2025, Series OO of 120,000 shares at 6.625% for $3.0 billion on April 29, 2025 with a $25,000 liquidation preference, Series UU of 100,000 shares at 6.250% for $2.5 billion on July 24, 2025 with a $25,000 liquidation preference, $2.7 billion of Series MM and AA redeemed, and the six-quarter/three-semi-annual arrears trigger for two additional directors terminating after four quarterly or two semi-annual payments) from its FY2025 Form 10-K, filed February 25, 2026. Holding-period rules from IRS Publication 550 and the dividends-received deduction from IRS Publication 542, both accessed September 3, 2026. The Dodd-Frank section 171 phase-out beginning January 1, 2013 and the permanent exemption for holding companies under $15 billion of consolidated assets from 12 U.S.C. 5371, 2023 edition. The March 19, 2026 joint proposals and the June 18, 2026 comment deadline from the Federal Reserve, FDIC and OCC press release; the article states explicitly that these are proposals rather than rules in force. Arithmetic recomputed rather than sourced: 15,103 shares retired (100,000 less 84,897); $8,000 per share per year as 8% of $100,000; 84,897 shares at $100,000 face giving roughly $8.5 billion; preferred at about eight cents per dollar of long-term debt at Bank of America (25,992/317,816); the 125 basis point step from Comerica's 5.625% to 6.875%; and $3.0 billion plus $2.5 billion giving the $5.5 billion issued. Not verified: why Occidental's $8,287 million carrying value sits below the roughly $8.5 billion of face value, which none of the cited filings explains in the excerpts available; and whether the 15,103 shares were retired under the mandatory trigger or otherwise. The claim that preferred yields less than debt of comparable risk from the same issuer, and that a corporate marginal buyer sets that price, is analysis rather than a sourced finding; none of the cited documents addresses relative yields.",
